(window.STEP_DATA = window.STEP_DATA || {})[26] = {"n":26,"set":"C","level":"Exam Standard","band":"B1 20% · B2 50% · B2+ 30%","sec":[{"id":"R","name":"Reading Comprehension","ar":"فهم المقروء","min":70,"from":1,"to":40,"g":[{"t":"Black holes","p":["Few objects in the universe capture the imagination like black holes. A black hole is a region of space where gravity is so strong that nothing, not even light, can escape from it. Although the idea once seemed like science fiction, astronomers now have strong evidence that black holes exist in large numbers, and in recent years, they have even produced images of them.","The basic idea is surprisingly old. In the eighteenth century, the English scientist John Michell suggested that a star might be so massive that light could not escape its gravity. However, the modern understanding of black holes comes from Albert Einstein's general theory of relativity, published in 1915, which describes gravity as the bending of space and time by mass. Shortly afterwards, the German physicist Karl Schwarzschild found a solution to Einstein's equations that described what we now call a black hole, although Einstein himself doubted that such objects could exist in nature.","Most black holes form when very large stars reach the end of their lives. A star shines because nuclear reactions in its core produce energy that pushes outwards, balancing the inward pull of gravity. When a massive star runs out of fuel, this balance is lost. The core collapses under its own weight, often in a few seconds, while the outer layers are thrown off in a tremendous explosion called a supernova. If the remaining core is more than about three times the mass of the Sun, nothing can stop its collapse, and a black hole is born.","The boundary around a black hole is called the event horizon. Anything that crosses it, whether light, gas or a spacecraft, can never return. Because no light escapes, black holes cannot be seen directly. Instead, astronomers detect them by their effects on their surroundings. When gas falls towards a black hole, it forms a swirling disc that becomes extremely hot and emits powerful X-rays. Astronomers can also observe stars orbiting an invisible object at great speed. By tracking stars near the centre of our galaxy for decades, two research teams showed that a black hole about four million times the mass of the Sun lies there, a discovery that earned a Nobel Prize in 2020.","In 2019, the Event Horizon Telescope project, which linked radio telescopes around the world to create a virtual telescope the size of the Earth, released the first image of a black hole's shadow, in a galaxy called M87. The image showed a bright ring of hot gas surrounding a dark centre, exactly as Einstein's theory predicted. Three years later, the same team released an image of the black hole at the centre of our own galaxy. Meanwhile, since 2015, detectors have recorded gravitational waves, tiny ripples in space produced when two black holes collide and merge.","Many mysteries remain. Scientists still do not fully understand how supermassive black holes, which can be billions of times heavier than the Sun, grew so large so early in the history of the universe. Nor do they know exactly what happens at the centre of a black hole, where current theories break down. The physicist Stephen Hawking also proposed that black holes slowly lose energy through a process now called Hawking radiation, but this effect is far too weak to have been observed. Black holes therefore remain one of the most important testing grounds for our understanding of the laws of physics."],"q":[{"n":1,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["the life of Albert Einstein","how telescopes are manufactured","the history of the Milky Way","what black holes are, how they form and how they are studied"],"k":3,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage covers these topics.","h":"الثقوب السوداء."},{"n":2,"s":"What can be inferred about Einstein's view of black holes?","o":["He discovered the first black hole.","He did not expect them to exist in reality.","He photographed a black hole.","He believed they were very common."],"k":1,"ty":"inference","e":"Einstein 'doubted that such objects could exist'.","h":"شك في وجودها."},{"n":3,"s":"Why does a massive star's core collapse at the end of its life?","o":["It becomes too hot.","It is struck by another star.","Its outer layers become heavier.","Nuclear reactions stop balancing the pull of gravity."],"k":3,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"اختلال التوازن."},{"n":4,"s":"The word 'tremendous' in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to","o":["extremely powerful","very quiet","slightly bright","quite slow"],"k":0,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"A supernova is a huge explosion.","h":"هائل."},{"n":5,"s":"What is the event horizon?","o":["the bright ring of gas around a black hole","the boundary beyond which nothing can return","the centre of a galaxy","a type of radio telescope"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 4 defines it.","h":"أفق الحدث."},{"n":6,"s":"How do astronomers detect black holes if they cannot see them directly?","o":["by observing their effects on nearby gas and stars","by listening to their sounds","by sending spacecraft into them","by measuring their colour"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 4 explains this.","h":"من آثارها على المحيط."},{"n":7,"s":"In paragraph 4, the word 'it' in 'it forms a swirling disc' refers to","o":["the black hole","gas","light","the event horizon"],"k":1,"ty":"reference","e":"Gas forms a disc.","h":"تعود على الغاز."},{"n":8,"s":"Why was the Event Horizon Telescope able to photograph a black hole's shadow?","o":["It was sent into space near M87.","It used X-rays instead of radio waves.","It linked telescopes around the world into one virtual telescope.","It was the largest single telescope ever built."],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 5 explains this.","h":"ربط التلسكوبات."},{"n":9,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as an unsolved question about black holes?","o":["how supermassive black holes grew so quickly","how to travel safely through them","what happens at their centre","whether Hawking radiation can be observed"],"k":1,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Travel is not mentioned.","h":"السفر عبرها غير مذكور."},{"n":10,"s":"Which statement best summarizes the passage?","o":["Black holes are well supported by evidence, but they still raise major questions.","Black holes are only a theory with no evidence.","Everything about black holes is now understood.","Black holes can easily be seen with ordinary telescopes."],"k":0,"ty":"summary / conclusion","e":"It summarizes the main points.","h":"أدلة قوية وأسئلة مفتوحة."}]},{"t":"Phoenician sailors and trade","p":["Around three thousand years ago, a group of city-states on the narrow coastal strip of what is now Lebanon became the greatest seafaring traders of the ancient Mediterranean. The Phoenicians, as the Greeks called them, lived in cities such as Tyre, Sidon and Byblos. Hemmed in by mountains to the east and with limited farmland, they looked to the sea for their prosperity.","The Phoenicians had an important natural resource: the cedar forests of the Lebanon mountains. Cedar wood was strong, fragrant and resistant to decay, making it ideal for shipbuilding and for the temples and palaces of neighbouring kingdoms. The Phoenicians built sturdy ships and became expert navigators. They are believed to have sailed at night using the stars, particularly the Pole Star, which the Greeks sometimes called the 'Phoenician star'.","Phoenician merchants traded a remarkable variety of goods. They exported cedar wood, glass, fine metalwork and, above all, a precious purple dye. This dye was produced from a type of sea snail called the murex. Thousands of snails were needed to make a small amount of dye, and the process produced a terrible smell, but the resulting colour did not fade easily. Purple cloth became so expensive that it was associated with kings and emperors, and the name 'Phoenician' itself may come from a Greek word referring to this reddish-purple colour.","To support their trade, the Phoenicians established a network of trading posts and colonies across the Mediterranean, from Cyprus and Sicily to Spain and North Africa. The most famous, Carthage, in modern Tunisia, was founded in the ninth century BCE and eventually became a great power in its own right, challenging Rome for control of the western Mediterranean. According to the Greek historian Herodotus, Phoenician sailors even circumnavigated Africa around 600 BCE on behalf of an Egyptian pharaoh, a journey that took about three years, though historians still debate whether this really happened.","Perhaps the Phoenicians' most lasting contribution was not a product but an idea: the alphabet. Earlier writing systems, such as Egyptian hieroglyphs and Mesopotamian cuneiform, used hundreds of signs, so that reading and writing were skills limited to trained scribes. The Phoenicians used a system of just twenty-two letters, each representing a consonant sound. This simple system was easy to learn and well suited to the practical needs of merchants. It was adopted and adapted by many other peoples. The Greeks added letters for vowels, creating the basis of the Greek and Latin alphabets, while other branches of the Phoenician system's family led, over many centuries, to the Aramaic and Arabic scripts.","Phoenician power declined from the sixth century BCE onwards, as their cities came under the control of larger empires, including the Babylonians, Persians and, later, Alexander the Great, who captured Tyre in 332 BCE after a long siege. Carthage was finally destroyed by Rome in 146 BCE. Yet the Phoenicians' influence survives every time someone reads or writes using an alphabet, a reminder that trade has always carried ideas as well as goods."],"q":[{"n":11,"s":"Which of the following is the best title for the passage?","o":["The Mountains of Lebanon","How Rome Defeated Carthage","The Science of Navigation","The Phoenicians: Traders Who Spread an Alphabet"],"k":3,"ty":"best title","e":"The passage focuses on Phoenician trade and the alphabet.","h":"الفينيقيون والأبجدية."},{"n":12,"s":"Why did the Phoenicians turn to the sea for their prosperity?","o":["Their cities were built on islands.","They were forbidden to travel by land.","They had limited farmland and were surrounded by mountains.","They had no natural resources at all."],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 1 explains this.","h":"قلة الأراضي الزراعية."},{"n":13,"s":"Why was cedar wood valuable?","o":["It was very light and cheap.","It produced a purple dye.","It grew in many countries.","It was strong and resistant to decay."],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 2 explains this.","h":"قوي ومقاوم للتلف."},{"n":14,"s":"What can be inferred from the name 'Phoenician star'?","o":["The Phoenicians were known for navigating by the Pole Star.","The Phoenicians discovered the Pole Star.","The star was visible only in Lebanon.","The Greeks did not use the stars."],"k":0,"ty":"inference","e":"The Greeks associated the star with them.","h":"اشتهروا بالملاحة بالنجم القطبي."},{"n":15,"s":"Why was purple cloth so expensive?","o":["It was made only in Carthage.","It was imported from India.","Thousands of snails were needed to make a small amount of dye.","It faded quickly and had to be replaced."],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"صعوبة الإنتاج."},{"n":16,"s":"The word 'circumnavigated' in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to","o":["conquered","mapped from the air","avoided","sailed all the way around"],"k":3,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"They sailed around Africa.","h":"أبحروا حول."},{"n":17,"s":"What does the writer suggest about the voyage around Africa?","o":["It was proven by Egyptian records.","It took only three months.","It is not certain that it actually took place.","It was led by the Greeks."],"k":2,"ty":"inference","e":"'historians still debate whether this really happened'.","h":"غير مؤكدة."},{"n":18,"s":"Why was the Phoenician alphabet easier to learn than earlier systems?","o":["It used pictures of objects.","It included hundreds of vowel signs.","It used only twenty-two letters.","It was written only by scribes."],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 5 explains this.","h":"عدد قليل من الحروف."},{"n":19,"s":"Which of these events happened LAST?","o":["Carthage was founded.","Alexander captured Tyre.","Phoenicians may have sailed around Africa.","Carthage was destroyed by Rome."],"k":3,"ty":"sequence","e":"146 BCE is the latest.","h":"١٤٦ ق.م هو الأخير."},{"n":20,"s":"What is the writer's main conclusion?","o":["The Phoenicians invented money.","Trade spreads ideas as well as goods.","Purple dye was their most important legacy.","Trade always leads to war."],"k":1,"ty":"claim / evidence","e":"Stated in the final sentence.","h":"التجارة تنقل الأفكار."}]},{"t":"Crowdfunding","p":["Imagine that you have an idea for a new product, a film or a community project, but no bank will lend you the money to make it happen. Until recently, many such ideas would simply have been abandoned. Today, a growing number of people are turning instead to crowdfunding: raising money by collecting relatively small contributions from a large number of people, usually through online platforms.","The basic principle is not new. In the nineteenth century, when the Statue of Liberty arrived in New York, there was no money to build the base on which it would stand. A newspaper publisher, Joseph Pulitzer, launched a campaign asking readers to donate, promising to print the name of every donor. More than 160,000 people contributed, many giving less than a dollar, and the base was completed. What the internet has added is speed and reach: a campaign can now attract supporters from all over the world within days.","There are several types of crowdfunding. In donation-based crowdfunding, people give money to support a cause, such as medical treatment or disaster relief, without expecting anything in return. In reward-based crowdfunding, supporters receive a reward, often the product itself once it has been made, or a small gift such as a T-shirt. Equity crowdfunding allows investors to buy a small share in a new company, hoping to profit if it succeeds, while lending-based crowdfunding involves loans that must be repaid. In some countries, including Saudi Arabia, regulators have introduced rules to license and supervise equity and lending platforms in order to protect investors.","Crowdfunding offers clear advantages for entrepreneurs. Besides providing money, a successful campaign proves that there is demand for a product before it is manufactured, reducing financial risk. It can also generate publicity and build a community of loyal early customers who provide feedback. Some products that began as crowdfunding campaigns, such as certain smartwatches and virtual reality headsets, went on to become major commercial successes.","However, crowdfunding is far from risk-free. Most campaigns fail to reach their target, and those that succeed often face unexpected difficulties. Studies of reward-based projects have found that a large proportion deliver their products late, and a minority never deliver at all, sometimes because the creators underestimated the costs of manufacturing and shipping. Because supporters are not legally buying a product in the usual sense, they may have little protection if a project fails. There have also been cases of outright fraud, where campaigners collected money with no intention of delivering anything.","For investors, equity crowdfunding carries particular risks. Most new businesses fail within a few years, and shares in small private companies cannot easily be sold. Experts therefore advise investors to invest only money they can afford to lose and to spread their investments across several projects. Despite these risks, crowdfunding has opened up new opportunities for creative people and small businesses, and it gives ordinary people a chance to support ideas they believe in. Like any financial tool, it works best when participants understand both its potential and its limits."],"q":[{"n":21,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["how crowdfunding works and its benefits and risks","how to build a statue","the history of New York newspapers","how banks decide to lend money"],"k":0,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage describes crowdfunding.","h":"التمويل الجماعي."},{"n":22,"s":"Why does the writer mention the Statue of Liberty?","o":["to describe American history in detail","to show that statues are expensive","to show that the idea of crowdfunding is not new","to criticize Joseph Pulitzer"],"k":2,"ty":"purpose","e":"It is an early example.","h":"مثال قديم."},{"n":23,"s":"According to the passage, what has the internet added to crowdfunding?","o":["the idea of donations","speed and reach","government control","lower prices for products"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 2 states this.","h":"السرعة والانتشار."},{"n":24,"s":"In which type of crowdfunding do supporters expect nothing in return?","o":["donation-based","reward-based","equity","lending-based"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"التبرع."},{"n":25,"s":"Why have some regulators introduced rules for equity and lending platforms?","o":["to stop all crowdfunding","to increase taxes","to help platforms make more profit","to protect investors"],"k":3,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 3 explains this.","h":"حماية المستثمرين."},{"n":26,"s":"How can a successful campaign reduce financial risk for entrepreneurs?","o":["It guarantees that the product will succeed.","It shows there is demand before production begins.","It removes the need to manufacture anything.","It provides free advertising forever."],"k":1,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 4 explains this.","h":"إثبات الطلب مسبقًا."},{"n":27,"s":"The word 'outright' in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to","o":["accidental","minor","legal","complete and obvious"],"k":3,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Clear, deliberate fraud.","h":"صريح."},{"n":28,"s":"In paragraph 5, the word 'they' in 'they may have little protection' refers to","o":["supporters","creators","products","studies"],"k":0,"ty":"reference","e":"Supporters may lack protection.","h":"تعود على الداعمين."},{"n":29,"s":"What advice do experts give to equity crowdfunding investors?","o":["invest only money they can afford to lose and spread it across projects","invest all their savings in one company","avoid all small businesses","sell their shares immediately"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 6 states this.","h":"تنويع الاستثمار."},{"n":30,"s":"What is the writer's attitude towards crowdfunding?","o":["completely negative","balanced, recognizing both opportunities and risks","uncritically enthusiastic","uninterested"],"k":1,"ty":"attitude / tone","e":"Both sides are presented.","h":"متوازن."}]},{"t":"Seed banks and biodiversity","p":["Deep inside a mountain on the remote Norwegian island of Spitsbergen, far inside the Arctic Circle, lies one of the world's most unusual storage facilities. The Svalbard Global Seed Vault holds more than a million samples of seeds from almost every country on Earth. Often described as a 'doomsday vault', it is designed to protect the world's crop diversity against disasters ranging from wars and natural catastrophes to the gradual loss of traditional crop varieties. The permanently frozen ground provides natural cooling, so the seeds would remain frozen even if the electricity failed.","Why is such diversity so important? Over thousands of years, farmers developed countless local varieties of crops, each adapted to particular soils, climates and diseases. Within a single crop, such as wheat or rice, there may be thousands of varieties with different characteristics. However, modern agriculture tends to rely on a small number of high-yielding varieties. According to the Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations, around three-quarters of crop genetic diversity was lost during the twentieth century as farmers abandoned traditional varieties.","This loss matters because genetic diversity is the raw material that plant breeders use to develop new varieties. A traditional variety that seems unproductive today may carry a gene for resistance to a disease or tolerance of drought that becomes vital in the future. History shows the danger of depending on too few varieties. In the 1840s, Ireland relied heavily on a single type of potato, and when a disease destroyed the crop, around a million people died in the resulting famine.","Seed banks aim to conserve this diversity. There are around 1,700 seed banks worldwide, most of them run by national governments or research institutions. Seeds are cleaned, dried to reduce their moisture content and stored in sealed packages at low temperatures, usually around minus eighteen degrees Celsius. Under these conditions, many seeds can remain viable for decades or even centuries. Svalbard serves as a backup for these collections: if a national seed bank is damaged, its samples can be returned. This happened in 2015, when researchers withdrew seeds to replace collections lost from a gene bank in Aleppo during the war in Syria.","Saudi Arabia has also recognized the importance of conserving plant genetic resources. National research centres have collected and stored seeds of wild plants and local crop varieties, including those adapted to the Kingdom's harsh desert conditions, which may prove valuable as the climate becomes hotter and drier.","Nevertheless, seed banks have limitations. Some plants, including many tropical trees and important crops such as coconuts and cocoa, produce seeds that cannot survive drying and freezing, so they must be conserved in other ways, such as living collections or tissue culture. Moreover, seeds stored in a freezer do not continue to evolve alongside changing pests and climates. For this reason, many scientists argue that seed banks should be combined with in situ conservation, which means protecting plants in their natural habitats and supporting farmers who continue to grow traditional varieties. Seed banks are an insurance policy, not a substitute for living diversity in fields and forests."],"q":[{"n":31,"s":"What is the passage mainly about?","o":["the role of seed banks in conserving crop diversity","the geography of Norway","how to grow potatoes","the history of the United Nations"],"k":0,"ty":"main idea","e":"The passage discusses seed banks.","h":"بنوك البذور."},{"n":32,"s":"Why is the Svalbard Global Seed Vault sometimes called a 'doomsday vault'?","o":["It contains dangerous seeds.","It will be destroyed one day.","It is open only during disasters.","It is designed to protect seeds against major disasters."],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 1 explains its purpose.","h":"حماية من الكوارث."},{"n":33,"s":"According to the passage, why has crop genetic diversity declined?","o":["Seed banks destroyed old seeds.","Wars destroyed most farms.","Farmers abandoned traditional varieties for a few high-yielding ones.","Traditional varieties became too expensive to store."],"k":2,"ty":"cause–effect","e":"Paragraph 2 explains this.","h":"التخلي عن الأصناف التقليدية."},{"n":34,"s":"Why does the writer mention the Irish potato famine?","o":["to describe Irish cooking","to explain how seed banks began","to show the danger of depending on too few varieties","to compare Ireland with Norway"],"k":2,"ty":"purpose","e":"One variety was destroyed by disease.","h":"خطر الاعتماد على صنف واحد."},{"n":35,"s":"The word 'viable' in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to","o":["frozen","expensive","dangerous","able to grow successfully"],"k":3,"ty":"vocabulary in context","e":"Seeds remain able to germinate.","h":"قابل للإنبات."},{"n":36,"s":"What happened in 2015?","o":["Seeds were withdrawn from Svalbard to replace a collection lost in Syria.","The Svalbard vault was built.","The vault was damaged by flooding.","Norway closed the vault to other countries."],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Paragraph 4 states this.","h":"سحب بذور لحلب."},{"n":37,"s":"What can be inferred about Saudi seeds adapted to desert conditions?","o":["They are no longer grown.","They cannot be stored.","They may become more useful as the climate changes.","They are only valuable in Norway."],"k":2,"ty":"inference","e":"The climate is becoming hotter and drier.","h":"قيمتها المستقبلية."},{"n":38,"s":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a limitation of seed banks?","o":["Some seeds cannot survive drying and freezing.","Storing seeds is illegal in some countries.","Stored seeds do not evolve with changing conditions.","They cannot replace diversity in natural habitats."],"k":1,"ty":"NOT / EXCEPT","e":"Legal issues are not mentioned.","h":"القانون غير مذكور."},{"n":39,"s":"In paragraph 6, 'in situ conservation' means","o":["storing seeds in freezers","growing plants in laboratories","protecting plants where they naturally grow","sending seeds to Norway"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"Defined in paragraph 6.","h":"الحفظ في الموطن."},{"n":40,"s":"What does the writer mean by calling seed banks 'an insurance policy'?","o":["They are paid for by insurance companies.","They provide protection in case of loss but do not replace living diversity.","They guarantee that no crops will be lost.","They are more important than farmers."],"k":1,"ty":"claim / evidence","e":"The last sentence explains this.","h":"حماية احتياطية."}]}],"i":"Read each passage and answer the questions that follow. Choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","iar":"اقرأ كل نص ثم أجب عن الأسئلة التي تليه، واختر الإجابة الصحيحة."},{"id":"S","name":"Sentence Structure","ar":"تركيب الجمل","min":35,"from":41,"to":70,"g":[{"i":"Choose the word or phrase that best completes the sentence.","err":false,"q":[{"n":41,"s":"Never before ______ so many people attend a university open day.","o":["had the staff seen","the staff had seen","the staff saw","saw the staff"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"had the staff seen — Never before: inversion.","h":"القلب بعد Never before."},{"n":42,"s":"If I had saved more money last year, I ______ a new laptop now.","o":["could have afforded","could afford","can afford","would have afford"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"could afford — Mixed conditional: past condition, present result.","h":"شرط مختلط."},{"n":43,"s":"______ the instructions carefully, he assembled the shelves in twenty minutes.","o":["Having followed","Followed","To follow","Being followed"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Having followed — Perfect participle clause.","h":"Having + p.p."},{"n":44,"s":"The teacher demanded that the student ______ immediately.","o":["apologizes","apologizing","to apologize","apologize"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"apologize — Subjunctive after demand that.","h":"صيغة الطلب."},{"n":45,"s":"The festival is expected ______ more than a million visitors next month.","o":["attracting","to attract","to have attracted","that it attracts"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"to attract — Passive reporting about the future: to + base verb.","h":"is expected to."},{"n":46,"s":"I wish my neighbours ______ so much noise every night.","o":["don't make","haven't made","won't make","wouldn't make"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"wouldn't make — wish + would for annoying habits.","h":"wish + would."},{"n":47,"s":"The reason I left early ______ that I had a headache.","o":["is being","was","were","because"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"was — The reason … was that.","h":"The reason was that."},{"n":48,"s":"Not once ______ late for a lecture during the whole year.","o":["Majed was","was Majed","did Majed was","Majed did be"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"was Majed — Not once: inversion with be.","h":"القلب بعد Not once."},{"n":49,"s":"The company employs two hundred people, half of ______ work from home.","o":["whom","them","who","which"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"whom — half of whom.","h":"half of whom."},{"n":50,"s":"______ we to lose this match, we would be out of the tournament.","o":["Had","Should","If","Were"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Were — Were at the start = if we were.","h":"Were بدل If."},{"n":51,"s":"The patient ______ for an hour before the doctor finally arrived.","o":["had been waiting","was waited","has been waiting","waits"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"had been waiting — Past perfect continuous.","h":"الماضي التام المستمر."},{"n":52,"s":"He resents ______ like a child by his older brothers.","o":["treating","to be treated","be treated","being treated"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"being treated — resent + passive gerund.","h":"resent + being + p.p."},{"n":53,"s":"The success of the project depends ______ the support of local communities.","o":["of","from","on","at"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"on — depend on.","h":"depend on."},{"n":54,"s":"______ you have a ticket, you won't be allowed into the stadium.","o":["Provided","As long as","Unless","In case"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Unless — Unless = if not.","h":"Unless."},{"n":55,"s":"Suppose your phone ______ during the exam. What would you do?","o":["rings","rang","will ring","has rung"],"k":1,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"rang — Suppose + past for an imaginary situation.","h":"Suppose + الماضي."},{"n":56,"s":"The new airport terminal ______ by the end of next year.","o":["will have been completed","will complete","is completing","has been completed"],"k":0,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"will have been completed — Future perfect passive.","h":"المستقبل التام المجهول."},{"n":57,"s":"______ the fact that he was exhausted, he finished the race.","o":["Although","Even though","Despite","However"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"Despite — Despite the fact that.","h":"Despite the fact that."},{"n":58,"s":"The coach reminded the players ______ their water bottles.","o":["bringing","bring","to bring","that bring"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"to bring — remind + object + to-infinitive.","h":"remind sb to."},{"n":59,"s":"The more carefully you plan, ______ mistakes you will make.","o":["fewer","the less","the fewest","the fewer"],"k":3,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"the fewer — Double comparative with countable nouns: fewer.","h":"the fewer."},{"n":60,"s":"We'd rather ______ the museum in the morning, when it's less crowded.","o":["visiting","to visit","visit","visited"],"k":2,"ty":"sentence completion","e":"visit — would rather + base verb.","h":"would rather."}]},{"i":"Choose the underlined part (A, B, C or D) that is incorrect.","err":true,"q":[{"n":61,"s":"Mathematics (A) is the subject (B) that I find (C) the more (D) difficult.","o":["is","that","the more","difficult"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"the more — Superlative needed: the most.","h":"التفضيل the most."},{"n":62,"s":"(A) Despite she was tired, she (B) finished (C) her (D) homework.","o":["Despite","finished","her","homework"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"Despite — A clause needs Although.","h":"Although مع الجملة."},{"n":63,"s":"Neither Ali (A) nor his brothers (B) were able to (C) attend the (D) meet.","o":["nor","were","attend","meet"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"meet — A noun is needed: meeting.","h":"الاسم meeting."},{"n":64,"s":"She (A) would (B) has finished earlier if she (C) had (D) started on time.","o":["would","has","had","started"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"has — would + have + past participle.","h":"would have."},{"n":65,"s":"He asked me (A) what time (B) the train (C) would (D) left.","o":["what","the","would","left"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"left — would + base verb: leave.","h":"would + المصدر."},{"n":66,"s":"My father (A) has (B) been a doctor (C) during twenty (D) years.","o":["has","been","during","years"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"during — Duration with present perfect: for.","h":"for مع المدة."},{"n":67,"s":"(A) Being rainy, we (B) decided to (C) stay (D) indoors.","o":["Being","decided","stay","indoors"],"k":0,"ty":"error identification","e":"Being — Dangling participle: As it was rainy.","h":"فاعل غير متطابق."},{"n":68,"s":"The (A) number of (B) applicants (C) were higher (D) than expected.","o":["number","applicants","were","than"],"k":2,"ty":"error identification","e":"were — The number … takes a singular verb: was.","h":"The number مفرد."},{"n":69,"s":"Not only (A) does he (B) speaks Arabic, but he (C) also (D) speaks Chinese.","o":["does","speaks","also","speaks"],"k":1,"ty":"error identification","e":"speaks — After does: base verb speak.","h":"does + المصدر."},{"n":70,"s":"I (A) look forward (B) to (C) hearing from you as soon as (D) possibly.","o":["look","to","hearing","possibly"],"k":3,"ty":"error identification","e":"possibly — as soon as possible.","h":"possible."}]}],"iar":"الأسئلة من ٤١ إلى ٦٠: اختر الكلمة أو العبارة التي تكمل الجملة إكمالًا صحيحًا."},{"id":"L","name":"Listening Comprehension","ar":"فهم المسموع","min":30,"from":71,"to":90,"g":[{"rec":1,"a":"M26-L-01","q":[{"n":71,"s":"What does the man mean by 'no walk in the park'?","o":["The exam was difficult.","The exam was held outdoors.","The exam was very short.","The exam was easy."],"k":0,"ty":"inference","e":"He describes difficult questions.","h":"الامتحان صعب."}],"t":"An idiom about a difficult exam","sc":["Situation: Two students are talking after an exam. (51 words)","Man: How did the statistics exam go?","Man: Honestly, it was no walk in the park. The last three questions were on topics we barely covered in class.","Man: I heard several people couldn't finish.","Man: I finished, but only just. I'm hoping the professor will take the difficulty into account when she marks it."]},{"rec":2,"a":"M26-L-02","q":[{"n":72,"s":"What does the first man want?","o":["to borrow the headphones","for the music to be quieter","to listen to the song again","help with his exam"],"k":1,"ty":"inference","e":"He hints the music is too loud.","h":"خفض الصوت."}],"t":"A hint to turn down music","sc":["Situation: Two flatmates are talking in the evening. (56 words)","Man: That's a great song you're playing. They must be able to hear it all the way down the street.","Man: Oh. Is it too loud?","Man: Well, I've got an exam at eight tomorrow, and I'm trying to memorize forty formulas.","Man: Sorry, I didn't realize. I'll use my headphones from now on. Good luck with the exam tomorrow."]},{"rec":3,"a":"M26-L-03","q":[{"n":73,"s":"How did the women feel about the lecture?","o":["They found it boring from beginning to end.","They understood everything easily.","They left before it finished.","They found part of it interesting and part of it hard to follow."],"k":3,"ty":"opinion","e":"First half fascinating, second half too technical.","h":"مشاعر مختلطة."}],"t":"Mixed reactions to a lecture","sc":["Situation: Two students are leaving a lecture hall. (62 words)","Woman: What did you think of the guest lecture on artificial intelligence?","Woman: The first half was fascinating, especially the examples from hospitals. But the second half was so technical that I got lost.","Woman: I felt the same. I understood the medical part, but not the mathematics.","Woman: I'll ask Dr. Rawan if she can share the slides. Maybe it'll make more sense on paper."]},{"rec":4,"a":"M26-L-04","q":[{"n":74,"s":"What is the second man doing?","o":["agreeing to share his notes","asking to borrow his friend's notes","complaining about the lectures","politely refusing to share his notes"],"k":3,"ty":"function","e":"He gives reasons and suggests an alternative.","h":"رفض دبلوماسي."}],"t":"A diplomatic refusal to share notes","sc":["Situation: Two classmates are talking. (72 words)","Man: Could I copy your lecture notes from the last three weeks? I've been really busy.","Man: I understand, but my notes are full of my own abbreviations. I'm not sure they'd make sense to anyone else.","Man: I'm sure I could work them out.","Man: To be honest, I think you'd find it more useful to watch the recorded lectures online. I can send you the links, and you can message me if anything's unclear."]},{"rec":5,"a":"M26-L-05","q":[{"n":75,"s":"What does the second woman really think about waiting?","o":["She is unhappy about the long wait.","She enjoys waiting in the sun.","She thinks the food will be excellent.","She wants to wait longer."],"k":0,"ty":"inference","e":"Her comment is ironic.","h":"سخرية من الانتظار."}],"t":"An ironic comment about a long queue","sc":["Situation: Two friends are waiting outside a new restaurant. (55 words)","Woman: We've been standing in this queue for forty-five minutes, and it's hardly moved.","Woman: Well, I'm sure it'll be worth it. Nothing says 'relaxing dinner' like standing in the sun for an hour.","Woman: Shall we just go somewhere else?","Woman: Yes, please. There's a quiet café round the corner that serves good sandwiches, and I'm absolutely starving."]},{"rec":6,"a":"M26-L-06","q":[{"n":76,"s":"How does the first man feel about his result?","o":["disappointed","angry","surprised and pleased","worried"],"k":2,"ty":"attitude / tone","e":"'I can't believe it.'","h":"متفاجئ وسعيد."}],"t":"Surprise at an exam result","sc":["Situation: Two students are checking their results. (52 words)","Man: I can't believe it. I got ninety-two in physics!","Man: Ninety-two? Weren't you saying last week that you'd probably failed?","Man: I was sure I'd made mistakes in the long questions. I must have done better than I thought.","Man: Congratulations! All those evenings you spent in the library obviously paid off. You should celebrate."]},{"rec":7,"a":"M26-L-07","q":[{"n":77,"s":"What does the manager actually want?","o":["for Faisal to cancel the meeting","for Faisal to update the client list","to update the list herself","to hire a new employee"],"k":1,"ty":"function","e":"She hints that he should do it.","h":"طلب غير مباشر."}],"t":"A request disguised as a suggestion","sc":["Situation: A manager speaks to an employee. (58 words)","Woman: Faisal, I was thinking it might be a good idea for someone to update the client list before Monday's meeting.","Man: That sounds sensible. Who did you have in mind?","Woman: Well, you're the one who knows the clients best.","Man: I see. All right, I'll have it ready by Sunday afternoon, and I'll send you a copy before the meeting."]},{"rec":8,"a":"M26-L-08","q":[{"n":78,"s":"Why does the woman ask for more time?","o":["She has another job offer.","She wants a higher salary.","She does not like the position.","She needs to discuss moving with her family. Longer conversations"],"k":3,"ty":"reason","e":"The job means moving to Riyadh.","h":"مناقشة الانتقال مع العائلة."}],"t":"A hesitation before accepting a job","sc":["Situation: A candidate speaks to an employer on the phone. (69 words)","Man: We'd like to offer you the position of project coordinator. Are you happy to accept?","Woman: Thank you so much. I'm very pleased. Could I possibly have a couple of days to think it over? The position would mean moving to Riyadh, and I need to discuss it with my family.","Man: Of course. Could you let us know by Thursday?","Woman: Yes, I'll call you on Thursday morning at the latest."]},{"rec":9,"a":"M26-L-09","q":[{"n":79,"s":"What does 'major revisions' mean, according to the first woman?","o":["The journal is interested but wants improvements.","The article has been rejected.","The article has been accepted without changes.","The reviewers disagree completely."],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"She explains it is common.","h":"اهتمام مع طلب تحسينات."},{"n":80,"s":"How will they respond to the first reviewer's comment?","o":["by collecting new data from other regions","by ignoring the comment","by explaining the limitation and softening their conclusions","by sending the article to another journal"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"They cannot collect new data.","h":"توضيح القيود."}],"t":"Discussing a peer review of an article","sc":["Situation: Two researchers are discussing reviewers' comments. (172 words)","Woman: We've received the reviewers' comments on our article about water use in agriculture. The journal says it could be accepted after major revisions.","Woman: Major revisions? That sounds serious.","Woman: It's actually quite common. It means they're interested but want us to improve some things. The first reviewer says our sample is too small: we only surveyed farms in one region.","Woman: That's fair, but we can't collect new data now. Could we explain the limitation and avoid making general claims?","Woman: I think so. We should add a paragraph on limitations and soften some of our conclusions. The second reviewer wants us to compare our findings with recent studies from other dry countries.","Woman: That's easy. I know several studies from Jordan and Morocco.","Woman: Good. The third reviewer only made minor comments about the figures. She wants clearer labels.","Woman: So, when do we have to resubmit?","Woman: Within two months. We also need to write a response letter explaining how we've dealt with each comment.","Woman: Let's divide the comments between us and meet again in two weeks."]},{"rec":10,"a":"M26-L-10","q":[{"n":81,"s":"How will the exhibition be organized?","o":["by following the route of the incense trade","by the age of the objects","by the material of the objects","in alphabetical order"],"k":0,"ty":"detail","e":"Visitors move along the route.","h":"حسب مسار التجارة."},{"n":82,"s":"Why does the man want to start arranging loans immediately?","o":["Loans usually take several months.","The national museum is closing.","The objects are very cheap.","The exhibition opens next week."],"k":0,"ty":"reason","e":"'Loans usually take several months to arrange'.","h":"تستغرق الإعارة وقتًا."}],"t":"Planning a museum exhibition on trade","sc":["Situation: Two museum staff are planning an exhibition. (176 words)","Man: Our new exhibition will be about the ancient incense trade through Arabia. How should we organize it?","Man: I suggest following the route itself. Visitors could start in southern Arabia, where frankincense and myrrh were harvested, and move north through oases like Najran, Al-Ula and Tayma.","Man: That's a nice idea. It would turn the exhibition into a journey.","Man: Exactly. Each section could show objects from that stop, such as inscriptions, pottery and coins.","Man: We should also include something interactive. Many visitors, especially children, lose interest in long text panels.","Man: What about a station where visitors can smell real frankincense and myrrh? And a touch screen map showing how long each stage of the journey took by camel.","Man: Excellent. We'll need to borrow some objects from other museums, though. Our own collection has very little from the south.","Man: I'll contact the national museum this week. Loans usually take several months to arrange, so we should start immediately.","Man: And let's keep the text panels short and bilingual, in Arabic and English.","Man: Agreed. No more than fifty words per panel."]},{"rec":11,"a":"M26-L-11","q":[{"n":83,"s":"Why does Lulwa object to the theme 'Sustainability'?","o":["It is too narrow for most faculties.","The faculty adviser rejected it.","Recent conferences have already focused on the environment.","Nobody is interested in the environment."],"k":2,"ty":"reason","e":"The last two conferences were about it.","h":"التكرار."},{"n":84,"s":"Why do they change the theme to 'Innovation for Communities'?","o":["to make it more about the environment","to attract international speakers","to reduce the number of presentations","to give presenters a clearer focus Talks and lectures"],"k":3,"ty":"purpose","e":"The original theme was too broad.","h":"تركيز أوضح."}],"t":"Choosing a theme for a student conference","sc":["Situation: Three students on an organizing committee are talking. (180 words)","Dana: We need to choose a theme for this year's student research conference. Any ideas?","Shahad: What about 'Sustainability'? It connects with so many subjects: engineering, business, biology, even literature.","Lulwa: It's a good theme, but the last two conferences were both about the environment. I think people might be tired of it.","Dana: That's true. How about 'Innovation in Saudi Arabia'?","Lulwa: I like that. It's broad enough for every faculty, and it connects with the many new projects in the Kingdom.","Shahad: But isn't it a bit too broad? Almost any topic could fit.","Dana: Maybe we could narrow it: 'Innovation for Communities'. Then research would need to show how ideas benefit people.","Shahad: That's better. It gives presenters a clear focus.","Lulwa: Agreed. Let's propose it to the faculty adviser tomorrow and prepare a short description for the call for papers.","Dana: We should also suggest a keynote speaker who works on community projects. That would attract more students.","Shahad: My cousin works at a social innovation centre in Riyadh. I could ask whether her director would be willing to speak.","Lulwa: Perfect. Let's include that in our proposal."]},{"rec":12,"a":"M26-L-12","q":[{"n":85,"s":"Which option is at the top of the waste hierarchy?","o":["recycling materials","burning waste for energy","preventing waste from being produced","disposing of waste in landfill"],"k":2,"ty":"detail","e":"Prevention is at the top.","h":"منع النفايات."},{"n":86,"s":"Why is landfill still common in many countries?","o":["It produces electricity.","It is cheap and simple.","It protects groundwater.","It is required by international law."],"k":1,"ty":"reason","e":"Stated in the lecture.","h":"رخيص وسهل."},{"n":87,"s":"What is the lecturer's main point at the end of the talk?","o":["Technology alone can solve the waste problem.","Landfills should be built in every city.","Public participation is essential for waste systems to succeed.","Recycling is too expensive to be useful."],"k":2,"ty":"gist","e":"'the success of any system depends on public participation'.","h":"مشاركة الجمهور."}],"t":"Extended lecture: how cities manage waste","sc":["Situation: An environmental studies lecturer speaks to students. (296 words)","Lecturer: Good morning. Today we'll look at one of the biggest challenges facing modern cities: managing solid waste. As cities grow and people consume more, the amount of rubbish increases rapidly. Worldwide, cities produce more than two billion tonnes of solid waste each year, and this figure is expected to rise significantly in the coming decades. Waste management experts often refer to the waste hierarchy, which ranks options from most to least desirable. At the top is prevention: reducing the amount of waste produced in the first place, for example by using less packaging. Next comes reuse, such as repairing items or using refillable containers. Then comes recycling, which turns materials like paper, glass, metal and some plastics into new products. Below that is energy recovery, burning waste in special plants to produce electricity. At the bottom is disposal in landfill. Unfortunately, in many countries, landfill is still the main method, because it is cheap and simple. However, landfills take up large areas of land, can pollute groundwater and release methane, a powerful greenhouse gas, as organic waste decomposes. One effective strategy is separating waste at its source. When households sort recyclable materials and food waste from other rubbish, recycling becomes cheaper and more efficient, and food waste can be turned into compost or used to produce biogas. Saudi Arabia has set ambitious targets as part of Vision 2030 to divert most of its municipal waste away from landfill, through recycling and waste-to-energy projects. Technology also plays a role: some cities now use bins with sensors that signal when they are full, allowing collection routes to be planned more efficiently. Ultimately, however, the success of any system depends on public participation. Without people's cooperation in reducing and sorting waste, even the most advanced facilities cannot work effectively."]},{"rec":13,"a":"M26-L-13","q":[{"n":88,"s":"What is the most common cause of tsunamis?","o":["strong winds","large undersea earthquakes","meteorite impacts","heavy rain"],"k":1,"ty":"detail","e":"Stated in the lecture.","h":"الزلازل البحرية."},{"n":89,"s":"Why might ships at sea not notice a tsunami?","o":["It travels too slowly.","Its height in deep water may be less than one metre.","It only occurs at night.","Ships are designed to absorb waves."],"k":1,"ty":"reason","e":"Stated in the lecture.","h":"ارتفاعها صغير في العمق."},{"n":90,"s":"What should people do if the sea suddenly withdraws?","o":["go to look at the exposed seabed","wait for an official announcement","move closer to the harbour","move to high ground immediately"],"k":3,"ty":"detail","e":"It is a critical warning sign.","h":"التوجه إلى مكان مرتفع."}],"t":"Lecture: how tsunamis travel","sc":["Situation: An earth sciences lecturer speaks to students. (295 words)","Lecturer: Good afternoon. Today's lecture is about tsunamis, a series of powerful ocean waves that can cause enormous destruction when they reach the coast. The word 'tsunami' comes from Japanese and means 'harbour wave'. Most tsunamis are caused by large undersea earthquakes, particularly at subduction zones, where one tectonic plate slides beneath another. When the seabed suddenly moves up or down, it displaces a huge volume of water above it, generating waves that spread out in all directions. Tsunamis can also be triggered by underwater landslides, volcanic eruptions and, very rarely, by meteorite impacts. In the deep ocean, a tsunami behaves very differently from ordinary wind-driven waves. Its wavelength, the distance between one wave crest and the next, can be hundreds of kilometres, and it travels extremely fast, often over seven hundred kilometres per hour, about the speed of a passenger jet. Yet in deep water, its height may be less than one metre, so ships at sea may not even notice it passing beneath them. Everything changes as the tsunami approaches the shore. As the water becomes shallower, the wave slows down, and the energy that was spread through the deep water column is compressed. The wave therefore grows dramatically in height, sometimes reaching more than ten metres. Often, the first sign of a tsunami is not a wave but the sea suddenly withdrawing, exposing the seabed. This is a critical warning, and people should move to high ground immediately rather than going to look. Today, networks of seabed pressure sensors and buoys detect tsunamis in the open ocean and send data by satellite to warning centres, which can alert coastal communities, often giving them precious minutes or even hours to evacuate. Education is equally important: knowing the natural warning signs has saved many lives."]}],"i":"You will hear 13 recordings: 8 short conversations (one question each), 3 longer conversations (two questions each) and 2 talks (three questions each). Read the questions before each recording, then choose the best answer.","iar":"ستسمع ثلاثة عشر تسجيلًا: ثماني محادثات قصيرة (سؤال لكل منها)، وثلاث محادثات أطول (سؤالان لكل منها)، وحديثين أو محاضرتين (ثلاثة أسئلة لكل منهما). اقرأ الأسئلة قبل بدء كل تسجيل."},{"id":"C","name":"Composition Analysis","ar":"التحليل الإنشائي","min":20,"from":91,"to":100,"g":[{"q":[{"n":91,"s":"Which sentence best fills the blank in the paragraph?","o":["Many people now check headlines on their phones several times a day.","Some phones are very expensive.","Newspapers are printed at night.","Many people enjoy crossword puzzles."],"x":["Smartphones have changed the way people read the news. ______ As a result, traditional printed newspapers have lost many readers."],"k":0,"ty":"complete the paragraph","e":"A cause is needed before 'As a result'.","h":"سبب قبل As a result."},{"n":92,"s":"Which sentence is the best topic sentence for the paragraph?","o":["Many villages are far from cities.","Doctors study for many years.","Telemedicine offers important benefits for patients and doctors.","Some patients dislike hospitals."],"x":["______ It allows patients in remote villages to consult specialists without travelling long distances. It also reduces waiting times and helps doctors monitor chronic conditions from a distance."],"k":2,"ty":"topic sentence / thesis","e":"The details list benefits of telemedicine.","h":"فوائد الطب عن بعد."},{"n":93,"s":"(A) Most of the (B) equipments in the laboratory (C) is (D) new.","o":["Most of","equipments","is","new"],"k":1,"ty":"error","e":"equipment is uncountable.","h":"equipment غير معدود."},{"n":94,"s":"Which sentence best fills the blank in the paragraph?","o":["Leopards have spotted coats.","Only a few hundred are believed to survive in the wild.","Some zoos are very large.","Cats are popular pets."],"x":["The Arabian leopard is one of the most endangered animals in the world. ______ Conservation programmes are now breeding leopards in captivity, with the aim of returning them to the wild."],"k":1,"ty":"complete the paragraph","e":"It explains the danger before the solution.","h":"توضيح الخطر."},{"n":95,"s":"Which sentence is the best topic sentence for the paragraph?","o":["There are many different ways to learn a new skill.","Videos can be very long.","Workshops are usually expensive.","Reading is a popular hobby."],"x":["______ Some people learn by watching videos, while others prefer reading manuals or attending workshops. Many find that a combination of methods works best."],"k":0,"ty":"topic sentence / thesis","e":"The details describe learning methods.","h":"طرق تعلم المهارات."},{"n":96,"s":"The professor (A) who (B) taught us last year (C) has (D) retire.","o":["who","taught","has","retire"],"k":3,"ty":"error","e":"Present perfect: has retired.","h":"التصريف الثالث retired."},{"n":97,"s":"Which sentence does NOT belong in the paragraph?","o":["Sentence 2","Sentence 3","Sentence 4","Sentence 5"],"x":["(1) Taif is famous for its roses. (2) Every spring, farmers harvest millions of rose flowers. (3) The petals are used to produce valuable rose oil and rose water. (4) Taif is also known for its pleasant summer weather. (5) Rose products from Taif are sold across the Gulf."],"k":2,"ty":"irrelevant sentence","e":"The weather does not develop the point about roses.","h":"الطقس خارج فكرة الورد."},{"n":98,"s":"Choose the best order for the sentences.","o":["2 – 4 – 1 – 3","2 – 1 – 4 – 3","1 – 2 – 4 – 3","2 – 1 – 3 – 4"],"x":["(1) Then, he joined a local football club for children.","(2) Salem first played football in the street with his friends.","(3) By the age of eighteen, he was selected for the national youth team.","(4) His talent was soon noticed by a professional coach."],"k":1,"ty":"best order","e":"first → Then → noticed → selected.","h":"التسلسل الزمني."},{"n":99,"s":"Which sentence is the best concluding sentence for the paragraph?","o":["Cars are expensive.","Some banks open early.","Many young people enjoy shopping.","For these reasons, financial education should begin at school."],"x":["Learning to manage money is an important skill for young people. Those who learn to budget and save early are less likely to fall into debt and more likely to achieve their goals, such as buying a car or a home. ______"],"k":3,"ty":"concluding sentence","e":"A logical conclusion.","h":"خلاصة منطقية."},{"n":100,"s":"Which word or phrase best fills the blank?","o":["Instead","Similarly","Moreover","For example Teacher Section — Mock 26"],"x":["The road to the village was closed because of flooding. ______, the doctors reached the village by helicopter."],"k":0,"ty":"transition / connector","e":"Instead shows an alternative action.","h":"Instead للبديل."}]}],"i":"Read each paragraph or set of sentences and choose the best answer (A, B, C or D).","iar":"اقرأ كل فقرة أو مجموعة جمل واختر الإجابة الصحيحة."}]};
